Externalize the Configuration of Applications
Objectives
Configure applications by using Kubernetes secrets and configuration maps to initialize environment variables and to provide text and binary configuration files.

Externalizing Application Configuration (Kubernetes)
1. What is Externalized Configuration?
In Kubernetes, applications run inside containers using images.
These images usually contain default configuration.
But in production, we often need to change configuration such as:
•	Database name
•	Database username
•	Passwords
•	API keys
•	Configuration files
Instead of modifying the container image every time, Kubernetes allows external configuration using:
1.	ConfigMaps → for normal configuration data
2.	Secrets → for sensitive data
This concept is called Configuration Externalization.
It makes applications:
•	Portable
•	Easy to manage
•	Secure
•	Environment independent
________________________________________
2. Deployment Configuration (YAML Example)
Example deployment snippet:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: hello-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: hello-deployment
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: hello-deployment
    spec:
      containers:
      - image: quay.io/hello-image:latest
        env:
        - name: ENV_VARIABLE_1
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: world
              key: hello
Explanation
Field	Meaning
apiVersion	Kubernetes API version
kind	Resource type (Deployment)
metadata	Resource name and labels
spec	Desired configuration
replicas	Number of pods
selector	Label selector
template	Pod configuration
containers	Container details
env	Environment variables
Here the environment variable is taken from a Secret.
________________________________________
3. ConfigMap (Configuration Map)
What is ConfigMap?
A ConfigMap stores non-sensitive configuration data as key-value pairs.
Used for:
•	Application settings
•	Configuration files
•	Environment variables
•	JSON configs
ConfigMaps help keep containers platform-independent.
________________________________________
Example ConfigMap
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: example-configmap
  namespace: my-app
data:
  example.property.1: hello
  example.property.2: world
  example.property.file: |
    property.1=value-1
    property.2=value-2
Sections
Section	Meaning
kind	ConfigMap resource
metadata	name and namespace
data	configuration values
________________________________________
binaryData
Used when storing binary files.
Example:
binaryData:
  bar: R0lGODlhAgACAIABAO4AAP///
This is Base64 encoded binary data.
________________________________________
4. Secrets
What is a Secret?
A Secret stores sensitive information.
Examples:
•	Passwords
•	API tokens
•	Database credentials
•	SSH keys
•	TLS certificates
Secrets are Base64 encoded (not encrypted by default).
________________________________________
Example Secret
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: example-secret
type: Opaque
data:
  username: bXl1c2VyCg==
  password: bXlQQDU1Cg==
Base64 decode example
echo bXl1c2VyCg== | base64 --decode
Output
myuser
________________________________________
5. Types of Secrets
Secret Type	Use
Opaque	Default secret (key-value)
Service Account Token	API authentication
Basic Authentication	username/password
SSH Keys	SSH login
TLS Certificates	HTTPS security
Docker Config	Image registry authentication
________________________________________
6. Creating Secrets (Commands)
Create secret from literals
oc create secret generic secret_name \
--from-literal key1=value1 \
--from-literal key2=value2
Example
oc create secret generic db-secret \
--from-literal username=admin \
--from-literal password=pass123
________________________________________
Create secret from files
kubectl create secret generic ssh-keys \
--from-file id_rsa=/path/id_rsa \
--from-file id_rsa.pub=/path/id_rsa.pub
________________________________________
Create TLS secret
oc create secret tls secret-tls \
--cert cert.crt \
--key key.pem
Used for HTTPS certificates.
________________________________________
7. Creating ConfigMaps
Using literals
kubectl create configmap my-config \
--from-literal key1=value1 \
--from-literal key2=value2
OpenShift shortcut:
oc create cm my-config \
--from-literal key1=value1
________________________________________
Creating from file
oc create configmap demo-map \
--from-file=httpd.conf
The file content becomes the value.
________________________________________
8. Using ConfigMaps as Environment Variables
Example Pod:
env:
- name: MYSQL_DATABASE
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef:
      name: config-map-example
      key: database.name
Explanation:
Field	Meaning
env	environment variables
configMapKeyRef	reference ConfigMap
name	ConfigMap name
key	key inside ConfigMap
________________________________________
Load all variables from ConfigMap
envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: config-map-example
This loads all keys as environment variables.
________________________________________
9. Using Secrets as Environment Variables
Example:
env:
- name: TEST_SECRET_USERNAME
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: test-secret
      key: username
Explanation:
Field	Meaning
secretKeyRef	reference secret
name	secret name
key	value inside secret
________________________________________
10. Mounting Secrets as Volumes
Instead of environment variables, secrets can be mounted as files.
Command:
oc set volume deployment/demo \
--add \
--type secret \
--secret-name demo-secret \
--mount-path /app-secrets
Result inside container:
/app-secrets/user
/app-secrets/root_password
Each key becomes a file.
________________________________________
11. Mounting ConfigMap as Volume
oc set volume deployment/demo \
--add \
--type configmap \
--configmap-name demo-map \
--mount-path /app-config
Now configuration files appear in:
/app-config/
________________________________________
12. Setting Environment Variables from Secrets
Command:
oc set env deployment/demo \
--from secret/demo-secret \
--prefix MYSQL_
Example:
Secret:
user
root_password
Environment variables created:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD
________________________________________
13. Updating Secrets or ConfigMaps
Step 1 — Extract data
oc extract secret/demo-secret \
--to /tmp/demo \
--confirm
Step 2 — Edit file
nano /tmp/demo/root_password
Step 3 — Update secret
oc set data secret/demo-secret \
--from-file /tmp/demo/root_password
________________________________________
14. Important Behavior
When updates happen
Method	Restart Required
Environment variables	Yes
Volume mount	No (auto updated)
Kubelet automatically updates mounted volumes.
________________________________________
15. Deleting Secrets and ConfigMaps
Delete secret:
kubectl delete secret/demo-secret
Delete configmap:
oc delete configmap/demo-map
________________________________________
16. Quick Comparison
Feature	ConfigMap	Secret
Purpose	Non-sensitive config	Sensitive data
Storage	Plain text	Base64 encoded
Examples	app configs	passwords
Security	low	restricted
